#
# prima-materia — Kabbalah
#
# Opened from the ordered harvest of prima-materia#924: registered at
# pm:corpusNamed in traditions/registry-west-asia.ttl with no node. The work
# opened is Ashlag's Petiḥa le-Ḥokhmat ha-Qabbala, read complete in the
# Hebrew; its harvest stands in traditions/kabbalah-petiha.ttl. The works the
# registration named and the further works of Ashlag listed in #924 stay
# unopened and are declared below.
#
# Order of the file: the work read; the unopened works; the tradition with the
# three decisions of SPEC §14.
#

#
# The work read
#

pmw:PetihaLeHokhmatHaQabbala a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Yehuda Leib Ashlag, Petiḥa le-Ḥokhmat ha-Qabbala, an opening to Kabbalistic wisdom"@en, "Jehuda Leib Aschlag, Petiḥa le-Ḥokhmat ha-Qabbala, Einleitung in die Weisheit der Kabbala"@de ;
    skos:note "Read complete on 2026-11-05 in the Hebrew, sections 1 to 188, in the text of the Sefaria library, version Zohar with Sulam commentary, Jerusalem 1945, whose version source Sefaria gives as the National Library of Israel record NNL_ALEPH001329911. The text layer has no title page; the edition is therefore taken from Sefaria's version record and not from a title page, and every node says so. Section 99 is absent from the numbering itself: the Hebrew passes from 98 to 100, and so does the Bnei Baruch English. The layer keeps the author's headings and his references to his diagrams (the Tree, figures 3 to 12) but not the diagrams. Cross-checked against the Hebrew of the Bnei Baruch edition, which keeps the abbreviations of the print."@en ;
    skos:note "Two translations by two translators. Avi Steinhart, The Sefaria Sulam (2023, CC-BY), covers sections 1 to 45 only. The English of Bnei Baruch, translator unnamed, copyrighted, read for understanding only, covers the whole work; it was taken from the Bnei Baruch media archive, content unit kB3eD83I. A German version of Bnei Baruch exists and was not used for the comparison. For sections 46 to 188 only one translator was reachable, and the nodes rest on the Hebrew; definitions are the harvest's own paraphrase, checked against both English texts for any shared run of six words."@en ;
    skos:note "Harvested under prima-materia#924 with two independent passes over the Hebrew and a reconciliation. The first pass wrote 265 records with full fields, the second 313 short ones. Ten records of the second pass had no counterpart in the first by offset within 1500 characters and a word overlap of at least a fifth; read at their places, one was owed and added (the summary of what is new in the world of Nekudim, §112), six were covered by records of the first pass at the same or the neighbouring section, and three were citations (of the Tikkunei Zohar, the Zohar Hadash and Scripture) that the author interprets and that are not owed. The second pass counted eleven receivings in all; every one is a citation of Talmud, Midrash, Zohar, Scripture or Sefer Yetzira read by the author, and none claims that the wording of the work comes from another speaker, so no pm:Receiving is written."@en ;
    skos:note "Harvest counts: 283 nodes, 208 concepts (among them 7 capacities, 17 conditions of failure, 1 sign of success, 1 dispute entered as a concept, and 18 nodes written by the integrator, 17 members of series and one added from the reconciliation), 3 practices, 6 yieldings, 9 prerequisites (7 at stated necessity, 2 at prescribed order), 7 cautions, 27 namings, 10 series and 13 orderings. One sign of success against seventeen conditions of failure. The one sign, the revelation of the King Messiah when the end of Atzilut comes to equal the end of Adam Kadmon, is announced for the future and is not a sign a practitioner could observe; most of the conditions of failure are cosmological — vessels that break, lights that depart — and six bear on human conduct: the husks and the wicked called dead for receiving in phase four itself (§16), a life wholly for oneself (§57), deeds of the lower ones that can blemish the back vessels of Zeʿir Anpin and Nukva (§126), Adam ha-Rishon's transgression (§151), the spoiling of deeds that makes the worlds descend again (§162), and the dependence of every light above the six extremities on deeds (§176). The work prescribes few practices: Torah and commandments done in order to give contentment to the Maker, the rehearsing of its three foundations, and good deeds that raise MaN."@en .

#
# Unopened works
#

