pmw:SeferHaBahir a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Sefer ha-Bahir"@en, "Sefer ha-Bahir"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run, which takes one work. Reachability not checked in this run."@en .

pmw:Zohar a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Zohar"@en, "Sohar"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run. The Petiḥa cites the Tikkunei Zohar at section 6 and the Zohar Hadash at section 32 and interprets both; those citations were read in the Petiḥa and the Zohar itself was not opened. The edition the Petiḥa was read in is the Zohar printing with Ashlag's Sulam commentary, of which the Petiḥa is the preface."@en .

pmw:PardesRimmonim a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Moses Cordovero, Pardes Rimmonim"@en, "Mose Cordovero, Pardes Rimmonim"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run, which takes one work. Reachability not checked in this run."@en .

pmw:EtzHayyim a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Ḥayyim Vital, Ez Ḥayyim"@en, "Chajim Vital, Ez Chajim"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run, which takes one work. The Petiḥa expounds its system without naming it; it names instead the author's own Panim Masbirot (section 1), which is not opened either. Reachability not checked in this run."@en .

pmw:ShaarHaGilgulim a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Ḥayyim Vital, Šaʿar ha-Gilgulim"@en, "Chajim Vital, Schaʿar ha-Gilgulim"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run, which takes one work. Reachability not checked in this run."@en .

#
# The tradition
#

pmt:Kabbalah a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PetihaLeHokhmatHaQabbala ;
    rdfs:label "Kabbalah"@en, "Kabbala"@de ;
    skos:definition "The medieval, early modern and modern Jewish transmission of the sefirot as the structure of emanation, and of the human act as bearing on it."@en ;
    skos:note "Contact route, the first of the three decisions SPEC §14 requires, kept at documented, and the route is in the text, though not under the names one would expect. The Petiḥa names no Lurianic author or work by title: Luria, Vital and Ez Ḥayyim do not occur in it. What it names is the Talmud (Makkot 23b) and Bereshit Rabba 44 in section 1, the Tikkunei Zohar in section 6, the Zohar Hadash in section 32, the Sefer Yetzira in section 100, and the author's own Panim Masbirot, Talmud Eser ha-Sefirot with Or Penimi, and the Tree printed at the end of his Beit Shaʿar la-Kavanot (sections 1, 20, 111, 168). Its whole vocabulary — Adam Kadmon and its partzufim, Akudim, Nekudim, the breaking of the vessels, Atik, Arikh Anpin, Abba ve-Imma, Zeʿir Anpin and Nukva, raising MaN — is the Lurianic system, and the work presents itself as an exposition of it. Ashlag's training among Hasidim is not named in the text. Any agreement between this file and the Zohar, Lurianic or Hasidic material of this holding, pmt:Hasidism included, is reception along that route and counts once."@en ;
    skos:note "Recension and edition, the second decision. The Hebrew is the text of the Sefaria library in the version Zohar with Sulam commentary, Jerusalem 1945 (version source given by Sefaria as NLI NNL_ALEPH001329911); the text layer carries no title page, so the edition rests on the version record. It was cross-read against the Bnei Baruch Hebrew, which keeps the abbreviations of the print."@en ;
    skos:note "The third decision, the number of citable places: 188 sections, numbered 1 to 188 with 99 absent from the numbering. Every node of traditions/kabbalah-petiha.ttl cites one section or a short run of them. Sections cited by no node, because they recapitulate, ask a question answered in the next section, or detail what a neighbouring node holds: 23, 36-37, 41, 61, 69-70, 73, 92, 95, 111, 114, 131-133, 136-137, 140, 145, 147, 157, and 99, which is empty. Sections 107, 164, 167, 169 and 171 have no node of their own and are cited only inside the run of an ordering or a series (the kings, §§104-109; the three ascents, §§163-173)."@en ;
    skos:note "What the reading found against the expectation of the registration's line. The Petiḥa is a systematic account of emanation from the first restriction to the worlds of Beria, Yetzira and Assiya, and almost none of it is a procedure: the human act enters at its beginning (§§1-12, 57-58), in passing at §§126 and 150-151, and at its end (§§141-176), and there it carries the work's few testable claims, that good deeds raise MaN and make the worlds rise, that spoiled deeds make them fall, and that the lights above the six extremities never become fixed for that reason. The orders it sets out are many and strict; ten are entered as series and thirteen as orderings, and none of the series names a consequence for skipping one of its places."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Sefer ha-Bahir; Zohar; Cordovero, Pardes Rimmonim; Vital, Ez Ḥayyim and Šaʿar ha-Gilgulim; Ashlag, Petiḥa le-Ḥokhmat ha-Qabbala, Haqdama le-Sefer ha-Zohar, Haqdama le-Talmud Eser ha-Sefirot, and the essays Matan Tora, Ha-Arvut, Ha-Shalom" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:PetihaLeHokhmatHaQabbala, pmw:HaqdamaLeSeferHaZohar, pmw:HaqdamaLeTalmudEserHaSefirot, pmw:MatanToraHaArvutHaShalom, pmw:SeferHaBahir, pmw:Zohar, pmw:PardesRimmonim, pmw:EtzHayyim, pmw:ShaarHaGilgulim ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Ashlag, Petiḥa le-Ḥokhmat ha-Qabbala, §§1, 6, 32, 100 and 168, Hebrew text of the Jerusalem 1945 printing with the Sulam commentary on the Zohar as given in the Sefaria library (edition from Sefaria's version record; no title page in the text layer)" .
